<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kasur is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the 18th century it was ruled by Pathan chiefs until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> annexed it around 1807. It is known as the burial place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet Bulleh Shah.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kasur is a city south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in the Pakistani province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the headquarters of Kasur District. Its name comes from the Arabic and Persian qasur, meaning palaces or forts. The city is an aggregation of fortified hamlets overlooking the alluvial valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> rivers. According to a legend, it was founded by Prince Kusha of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>, but historical records reject an ancient founding and date it to 1525, when it began as a fortified settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was agricultural, with forests, in the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisation</a>, and was ruled in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> and others. In 1005 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> took over the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> missionaries sent there made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> predominantly Muslim. The Kheshgi tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>, migrating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1525 in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, built small forts and established the Kheshgi chieftaincy. Kasur grew into a town of fortified hamlets called kots, and under Mughal rule it flourished in commerce and trade. It became the home of the Sufi saint and poet Bulleh Shah, who is buried in a large shrine there.</p><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> the region fell into a power vacuum, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> captured Kasur. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> sacked the city in 1747 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> and again in 1763. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> captured it in 1807 in the Battle of Kasur. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> the city was occupied by Company forces on 10 February 1846. Under British rule canals irrigated large areas of the district, and riots in April 1919 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> destroyed civic infrastructure, including the railway station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the formation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> of Kasur migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, while Muslim refugees from India settled there. The population is overwhelmingly Punjabi-speaking, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> the second language. Kasur emerged after independence as a major centre of leather tanning, and it remains known as the burial place of Bulleh Shah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kasur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Ranjit Singh annexed <b>Kasur</b> around 1807, he compensated its Pathan ruling family with the lands of Mamdot on the south bank of the Sutlej.</blockquote><a href="/state/mamdot-state">Mamdot State</a></li></ul></section>
